Here is the same AWS architecture written as simple text so you can understand it easily.

VPC
│
├── CIDR: 10.0.0.0/22 (1024 IP addresses)
│
├── Internet Gateway (IGW)
│
└── Public Route Table (PUBLIC-RT)
    │
    ├── Public Subnet 1 (PUBLIC-SN1)
    │   ├── CIDR: 10.0.0.0/25
    │   └── Load Balancer
    │
    ├── Public Subnet 2 (PUBLIC-SN2)
    │   ├── CIDR: 10.0.0.128/25
    │   └── NAT Gateway
    │
    └── Private Route Table (PRIVATE-RT)
        │
        ├── Private Subnet 1 (PRIVATE-SN1)
        │   ├── CIDR: 10.0.1.0/25
        │   └── App Server
        │
        ├── Private Subnet 2 (PRIVATE-SN2)
        │   ├── CIDR: 10.0.1.128/25
        │   └── App Server
        │
        └── Private Subnet 3 (PRIVATE-SN3)
            ├── CIDR: 10.0.2.0/24
            └── App Server

Data Flow (Very Simple)
========================

Internet
   │
   ▼
Internet Gateway (IGW)
   │
   ▼
Public Route Table
   │
   ├── Load Balancer
   │       │
   │       ▼
   │  Private Route Table
   │       │
   │       ├── App Server 1
   │       ├── App Server 2
   │       └── App Server 3
   │
   └── NAT Gateway
           │
           ▼
      Internet Access for Private App Servers

In one sentence

InternetGateWay(IGW) → Connects the Virtual Private Cloud(VPC) to the Internet.
Public Route Table → Used by public subnets.
Load Balancer → Receives user requests and forwards them to the app servers.
NAT Gateway → Allows private app servers to access the Internet without being publicly accessible.
Private Route Table → Used by private subnets.
Private Subnets → Contain the application servers, which are protected from direct Internet access.


